(a) This article shall not apply to new private deep wells to be drilled and used as a limited water supply, pursuant to the provisions and conditions of this section.
(b) The term "developer" means a person who desires to drill and use a private deep well as a limited water supply for the purposes of servicing newly constructed residential, commercial, or industrial buildings on properties owned by said person. The term "person" may refer to a natural person or corporate or other entity.
(c) No new private deep well shall be permitted within the city, except when the city is unable to supply water upon request for water service, and when, upon petition by the landowner, the city council, at its discretion, determines that a new private deep well is needed to service newly constructed residential, commercial, or industrial buildings located within the corporate limits of the city, and then only if the following conditions are satisfied:
(1) A developer shall make a well application and permit request on applications provided by the city. Said application shall include the following items:
a. A site plan of the property, showing lots to be serviced and the location of the well and distribution lines for water service;
b. An estimate from a well-drilling company, including its proposal for construction and specifications;
c. Proof of approval by the state department of natural resources, the county health department, and any other requited agency or entity as to permission to drill said proposed well.
(2) Contemporaneous with making its well application and permit request, the developer shall request to appear before the city council at a regularly scheduled meeting and be placed on the agenda for said meeting. The city council shall either approve or deny the well request. As a condition of approval, the developer must agree to execute an agreement between itself and the city, delineating the responsibilities and limitations of the developer aid the city as to water service provided by said private deep well, including, but not limited to, the following matters:
a. A legal description and visual depiction of the property to be serviced by said well;
b. The responsibility of the developer to install water supply and distribution lines from said well to the properties to be served by said well;
c. A plan of distribution lines for water supplied by said well;
d. Agreement of the developer to install sufficient electrical service for the well on a separate electric meter which only services the well;
e. Agreement of the developer to drill said well and install said distribution lines in full compliance with all state, federal and local laws in effect at the time of construction;
f. Agreement of the developer to drill said well and install said distribution lines at its sole cost and expense;
g. Ownership of the new private well;
h. Agreement of the developer to immediately convey all of its interest in the land, the well, the distribution system, the pipelines, the electrical service, and all other parts, accessories and appurtenances in fee simple to the city, at no charge or cost to the city, whereupon the same shall become and thereafter be a part of the public water system of the city, upon 30 days' notice from the city;
i. Acknowledgement of the developer that in the event the city is limited in its ability to supply all requests for water service, developer may continue operate said well under the terms conditions and limitations of its agreement with the city;
j. Duration of the new private deep well as a non-irrigation well and the timeframe for converting the well into an irrigation well;
k. Agreement of the developer:
1. To allow the city to have full and completely unrestricted access to the well and the entire distribution system for all lawful purposes, including, but not limited to, maintenance, meter reading and/or inspection; and
2. To provide easements sufficient in size for the city to conduct such activities;
l. Agreement of the city to be solely responsible for the operation and maintenance of the well and distribution system and payment of all costs for the operation of the same;
m. Agreement of the developer to pay a one-time nonrefundable maintenance fee per lot being serviced by the well;
n. Agreement of the city to charge to all properties connected to the well or distribution system monthly usage or water and/or sewer fees as set forth in the then-current schedule for utility fees in the city in the amounts established by city council;
o. Agreement of the developer that it is not entitled to any part of such monthly usage or water and/or sewer fees;
p. Indemnification of the parties;
q. Agreement and acknowledgment of the parties that the developer has not been granted authority or permission to operate any portion of the public water or utility system of the city;
r. Any other terms or conditions that the city council in its discretion deems necessary.
(3) The new deep private well shall be metered with all service connection.
(4) The new deep private well shall be limited in capacity to 99,999 gallons per day.
(5) The new deep private well shall provide water to no more than ten service connections at any one time.
(6) The new deep private well shall provide water to no more than 24 individuals at any one time.
(7) No private wells shall be permitted to be connected to the city's water system.
(8) Any existing well which is tied into the city's water system shall be disconnected within 20 days at the expense of the owner of the well after receiving written notice of the intention of the city to disconnect said well. If the city finds any private well connected after the expiration of the 20-day notification period, the city shall disconnect the private well at the expense of the owner of the well and shall also levy a fine of $1,000.00 against the owner of the property.
(Code 2011, § 4-4-10; Ord. of 10-1-1998, § L; Amd. of 10-11-2005, § L)

