(a) Ordinary maintenance or repair. Ordinary maintenance or repair of any exterior architectural or environmental feature in or on a historic property to correct deterioration, decay, or to sustain the existing form, and that does not involve a material change in design, material or outer appearance thereof, does not require a certificate of appropriateness.
(b) Failure to provide ordinary maintenance or repair. Property owners of historic properties or properties within historic districts shall not allow their buildings to deteriorate by failing to provide ordinary maintenance or repair. The commission shall be charged with the following responsibilities regarding deterioration by neglect:
(1) The commission shall monitor the condition of historic properties and existing buildings in historic districts to determine if they are being allowed to deteriorate by neglect. Property owners of historic properties or properties within historic districts shall preserve protected buildings or structures against decay and deterioration by being kept free from the following structural defects by the owner and any other person who may have legal custody and control thereof:
a. Deteriorated or inadequate foundation which jeopardizes its structural integrity;
b. Defective or deteriorated floor supports or any structural members of insufficient size to carry imposed loads with safety which jeopardize its structural integrity;
c. Members of walls, partitions or other vertical supports that split, lean, list, or buckle due to defective material or deterioration which jeopardize its structural integrity;
d. Structural members of ceilings and roofs, or other horizontal structural members which sag, split, or buckle due to defective materials or deterioration or of insufficient size to carry imposed loads with safety which jeopardize structural integrity;
e. Fireplaces or chimneys which list, bulge, or settle due to defective material or deterioration or are of insufficient size or strength to carry imposed loads with safety which jeopardize structural integrity;
f. Lack of weather protection which jeopardizes the structural integrity of the walls, roofs, or foundations;
g. Any other defect or condition in the building or structure which threatens its structural integrity.
(2) In the event the commission determines a failure to provide ordinary maintenance or repair to prevent or correct defects as set forth in subsection (b)(1) of this section, the commission will by registered or certified mail notify the owner of the property at the last address of said owner on record with the county tax commissioner, and set forth in writing with reasonable particularity the steps which need to be taken to remedy the situation. The owner of such property will have 30 days in which to take some remedial action, so that such structure or building shall be preserved and protected in accordance with the purposes of this chapter, or to make a claim to the commission of undue economic hardship or other undue hardship (i.e., exceptional practical hardship) to undertake such remedial steps.
(3) In the event that the condition is not remedied in 30 days or a claim of undue economic hardship or other undue hardship made, the owner shall be punished as provided herein this chapter and, at the direction of the city council, the commission may perform such maintenance or repair as is necessary to prevent demolition by neglect. The owner of the property shall be liable for the cost of such maintenance and repair performed by the commission. Labor and materials shall be a lien against the property and draw interest at the highest legal rate, the amount to be amortized over a period of ten years subject to a public sale if there is a default in payment.
(c) Affirmation of existing building and zoning codes. Nothing in this chapter shall be construed to exempt property owners from complying with existing city building and zoning codes, nor prevent any property owner from making any use of this property not prohibited by other statutes, ordinances or regulations.
(d) Undue hardship. The provisions set forth in section 16-5(g) shall apply in equal force to this section, provided solely as to undue hardship (economic or exceptional practical hardship) claims made under this section, the following provisions shall apply:
(1) The commission shall schedule a hearing requested under this section at its next regular meeting, within the limitations of its procedures for application deadlines.
(2) In the event of a finding of no undue economic hardship, the commission shall proceed with its efforts to seek remedial action from either the property owner or on its own accord as directed by the city council, as set forth in subsection (b)(3) of this section.
(Code 2011, § 8-1-6; Ord. of 7-11-2006, § VI)
